# XCU localedef
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Whether new locales can be created is implementation-defined (the
# POSIX2_LOCALEDEF symbol); every test reports NOTINUSE when getconf does not
# report a positive value for it. The locale built is a copy of the POSIX
# locale in each category, stored under a pathname inside the test's
# directory, and removed afterwards.

assertion  XCU.localedef.001
anchor     utilities/localedef.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/localedef.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/localedef.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/localedef.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
says       localedef -c -i FILE NAME, with FILE holding copy directives for
           the six categories from the POSIX locale and NAME a pathname with
           a slash, exits with status zero or one and the locale exists at
           NAME afterwards.
test       t/001-create-from-file.sh

assertion  XCU.localedef.002
anchor     utilities/localedef.html#STDIN
anchor     utilities/localedef.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       Without -i the source definitions are read from standard input,
           with the same result as in the -i case.
test       t/002-create-from-stdin.sh

assertion  XCU.localedef.003
anchor     utilities/localedef.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/localedef.html#CONSEQUENCES OF ERRORS
anchor     utilities/localedef.html#EXIT STATUS
class      disputed
says       When the -i file does not exist localedef exits with a status above
           zero and creates nothing at NAME.
note       disputed (Q-203): an error is to leave no permanent output, and a name
           with a slash is the pathname where a locale is stored, but the
           text does not say whether an empty directory left at that
           pathname is output; it is accepted here, anything with content
           is not.
test       t/003-missing-source.sh

assertion  XCU.localedef.004
anchor     utilities/localedef.html#CONSEQUENCES OF ERRORS
anchor     utilities/localedef.html#EXIT STATUS
class      disputed
says       A source that uses, in a category other than LC_CTYPE and
           LC_COLLATE, a symbolic character name that the character map does
           not define is an error: localedef exits with a status above zero
           and creates no permanent output at NAME.
note       disputed (Q-203): an error is to leave no permanent output, and a name
           with a slash is the pathname where a locale is stored, but the
           text does not say whether an empty directory left at that
           pathname is output; it is accepted here, anything with content
           is not.
test       t/004-broken-source.sh

assertion  XCU.localedef.005
anchor     utilities/localedef.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/localedef.html#OPTIONS
class      untestable
says       -f names a charmap that gives encodings to symbolic characters and
           -u a target codeset for ISO/IEC 10646 position values.
reason     The effect is visible only through the use of the created locale by
           other utilities and the charmap and codeset names are
           implementation-defined.
